// docs/index.html §9 (Ordering) on the net.  Nothing here is a method: each entry is one statement of §9, and the
// declaration resolves by following them.  Elements are numbers; a comparison is the net's OP2 `<=`, so the ledger's
// OP2-NUM-NUM is exactly the count of comparisons made (9.9), and every other interaction is a step (9.7).
//
// 9.11 the shape is the numeral: the input arrives indexed, as a binary tree; a balanced tree is the binary
// representation of n, a right chain is the tally.
@lcat = λ{#Nil: λys. ys; #Cons: λh. λt. λys. #Cons{h, @lcat(t, ys)}}
@A4 = #Node{#Node{#Leaf{2}, #Leaf{0}}, #Node{#Leaf{3}, #Leaf{1}}}
@A8 = #Node{#Node{#Node{#Leaf{5}, #Leaf{2}}, #Node{#Leaf{7}, #Leaf{0}}}, #Node{#Node{#Leaf{3}, #Leaf{6}}, #Node{#Leaf{1}, #Leaf{4}}}}
@C8 = #Node{#Leaf{5}, #Node{#Leaf{2}, #Node{#Leaf{7}, #Node{#Leaf{0}, #Node{#Leaf{3}, #Node{#Leaf{6}, #Node{#Leaf{1}, #Leaf{4}}}}}}}}
// 9.2 / 9.8 the fibre is the relabellings, and over a binary-indexed set it factors: the rearrangements of a node are
// the interleavings of the rearrangements of its halves — one fresh coordinate per interleaving choice, Π C(a+b,a) = n!
// points in all.  This is the coordinate over `perm A B`.
@rearr = λ{#Leaf: λ&a. #Cons{a, #Nil}; #Node: λ&l. λ&r. @inter(@rearr(l), @rearr(r))}
@inter = λ{#Nil: λ&y. y; #Cons: λ&h. λ&t. λ{#Nil: #Cons{h, t}; #Cons: λ&h2. λ&t2. &(?){#Cons{h, @inter(t, #Cons{h2, t2})}, #Cons{h2, @inter(#Cons{h, t}, t2)}}}}
// 9.6 Sorted, with the comparisons 9.3 says are implied never made: adjacent, by transitivity.
@sorted = λ{#Nil: 1; #Cons: λ&h. λ{#Nil: 1; #Cons: λ&h2. λ&t. (λ{1: @sorted(#Cons{h2, t}); 0: 0})(h <= h2)}}
@keep = λ&B. (λ{1: B; 0: &{}})(@sorted(B))
// 9.3 a comparison is a restriction: on an interleaving of two sorted runs the coordinate at the two heads is
// determined by their comparison (1 keeps "x first", 0 "y first"), and 9.6: Sorted on such an interleaving unfolds
// to exactly that comparison — the inner premises are held by the runs, not remade.  So the restricted line is one
// point, and 9.15: the fibre at a node is the restricted interleaving of the fibres of its halves.
@interS = λ{#Nil: λ&y. y; #Cons: λ&h. λ&t. λ{#Nil: #Cons{h, t}; #Cons: λ&h2. λ&t2. (λ{1: #Cons{h, @interS(t, #Cons{h2, t2})}; 0: #Cons{h2, @interS(#Cons{h, t}, t2)}})(h <= h2)}}
@sort = λ{#Leaf: λ&a. #Cons{a, #Nil}; #Node: λ&l. λ&r. @interS(@sort(l), @sort(r))}
// three readings of the same declaration, for the ledger: the whole line cut once by Sorted (9.2 alone), and the
// declaration followed through 9.6/9.15 on the balanced numeral and on the tally
@main = #Pair{%(@keep(@rearr(@A4))), #Pair{@sort(@A8), @sort(@C8)}}
